Setup. Spin-density-functional theory (SDFT) extends Kohn–Sham density-functional theory by treating, in addition to the electron density n(r), the vector spin magnetization m(r) = (m_x(r), m_y(r), m_z(r)) as a fundamental variable. The ground-state energy is written as a functional E[n, m] = T_s[n,m] + ∫ v_ext(r) n(r) dr − ∫ B_ext(r)·m(r) dr + E_H[n] + E_xc[n, m], with T_s the noninteracting kinetic energy, v_ext and B_ext external scalar and magnetic potentials, E_H the Hartree energy, and E_xc the exchange–correlation (xc) functional. Variation with respect to the 2×2 spin-density matrix yields Kohn–Sham equations with an effective scalar potential v_s(r) and an effective magnetic field B_s(r) = B_ext(r) + B_xc(r), where the xc magnetic field is B_xc(r) := −δE_xc[n,m]/δm(r).

A spin configuration is called noncollinear if m(r) is not everywhere parallel to a single fixed axis. In the local-spin-density approximation (LSDA), one takes E_xc^{LSDA}[n,m] = ∫ ε_xc^{hom}(n(r), |m(r)|) dr, which forces B_xc(r) ∥ m(r) pointwise. Standard noncollinear SDFT implementations impose this parallelity at every point, so that the local xc torque m(r) × B_xc(r) vanishes. A generalized-gradient approximation (GGA) takes the form E_xc^{GGA}[n,m] = ∫ f(n, m, ∇n, ∇m) dr; in general such functionals produce B_xc(r) that is not parallel to m(r), so the parallelity constraint is inconsistent with GGA. As an alternative, one may reformulate SDFT in terms of the off-diagonal element (the "staggered density") of the one-body spin-density matrix, but this requires constructing new approximations of E_xc as a functional of that variable and solving a more complicated Kohn–Sham system.

Problem. Provide a theoretically consistent and computationally practical formulation of noncollinear spin-density-functional theory beyond LSDA. Specifically, identify a class of xc functionals E_xc[n,m] (or a reformulation in terms of an equivalent set of variables such as the staggered density) together with associated Kohn–Sham equations that simultaneously satisfies all of the following requirements:
(i) reduces to the standard collinear SDFT when m(r) is everywhere parallel to a fixed axis;
(ii) is rotationally invariant in spin space (and, in the presence of spin–orbit coupling, jointly under spin–space rotations consistent with the underlying Hamiltonian);
(iii) is compatible with gradient-dependent (GGA-type) approximations without imposing the unphysical pointwise constraint B_xc(r) ∥ m(r);
(iv) yields physically correct local xc torques m(r) × B_xc(r) and the correct global zero-torque sum rule ∫ m(r) × B_xc(r) dr = 0;
(v) is numerically stable, in particular in regions where |m(r)| is small or vanishes;
(vi) is computationally tractable for realistic inhomogeneous many-electron systems.

Determine the optimal such formulation, or prove that no formulation can satisfy all of (i)–(vi) simultaneously.

